# An Interactive Journey up the Information Ladder

The Euler–Mascheroni constant can be read as the total information accumulated while moving through exponential waiting-time models of rates $1,2,3,\ldots$. Begin with the live ladder: increase the number of transitions and watch the positive partial sum rise toward $\gamma$.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The same computation is available as transparent Python. It evaluates each step stably with `log1p` and checks the telescoping identity against $H_n-\log(n+1)$.

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the finite identity and its proof</summary>
For $g_k=1/(k+1)-\log((k+2)/(k+1))$, the reciprocal terms sum to $H_n$, while the logarithms telescope because $\prod_{k=0}^{n-1}(k+2)/(k+1)=n+1$. Hence $\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}g_k=H_n-\log(n+1)$. Taking the limit gives $\gamma$.
</details>

## Why every step is positive

The inequality $\log x\le x-1$ gives nonnegativity of exponential KL divergence. Equality occurs only when the rates match, so every consecutive-rate step is strictly positive. Read more about [Kullback–Leibler divergence](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kullback%E2%80%93Leibler_divergence) and the [exponential distribution](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exponential_distribution).

{{algorithm:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

## Directed information and an exact square

KL divergence remembers direction. Explore how $D(a\|b)$ and $D(b\|a)$ differ, then observe that their sum always matches $(a-b)^2/(ab)$.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the symmetrization calculation</summary>
Adding $D(a\|b)=\log(a/b)+b/a-1$ and $D(b\|a)=\log(b/a)+a/b-1$ cancels the logarithms. A common denominator turns the remainder into $(a-b)^2/(ab)$.
</details>

{{visualization:2}}

## A microscope for quadratic decay

At large $k$, the relative rate change is small. The divergence is second order in that change, and each term lies below two explicit rational envelopes.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the logarithmic inequality behind the bound</summary>
For $x>0$, define $f(x)=\log(1+x)-2x/(2+x)$. Since $f(0)=0$ and $f'(x)=x^2/((1+x)(2+x)^2)>0$, one has $\log(1+x)\ge2x/(2+x)$. Substituting $x=1/(k+1)$ yields $g_k\le1/((k+1)(2k+3))\le1/(2(k+1)^2)$.
</details>

## Continue the investigation

Use the scale and symmetry diagnostic to test arbitrary rates, then ask what changes for polynomial or geometric rate chains. Background on the constant itself is available in the [Euler–Mascheroni constant overview](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euler%27s_constant).

